DIAGNOSTIC: dump the CSID register space on stop

WHY
---
At this point going register by register is a waste of time: EVERYTHING the
vendor writes has been verified one by one -CSIPHY lane table 115 of 115, common
block, reset sequence, interrupt masks, CSID configuration read back bit by bit,
sensor tables 244 of 244 and 66 of 66- and it matches.

What is missing is what is NOT written and is not known to exist.

So the whole CSID space (0x000 to 0x400) is dumped when the stream is stopped.
What matters most are the registers that mainline never reads and that
Qualcomm's maps declare:

    0x350   csid_rdi_status          state of the RDI path
    0x354   csid_rdi_misr_val0       signature of the received data
    0x358   csid_rdi_misr_val1       (they would be non-zero if a SINGLE piece
    0x35c   csid_rdi_misr_val2        of data had gone through the RDI)
    0x360   csid_rdi_misr_val3
    0x160   total packets received
    0x168   total CRC errors

The MISR is especially useful: mainline ENABLES it in CSI2_RX_CFG1 (bit 6) and
then never reads it. It is an accumulated signature of what goes through the
path; if it comes out non-zero, something has gone through even if the counters
say it has not.

It goes behind a parameter because it is 128 reads and 32 lines of register.

This does NOT fix anything: it is to look at everything at once instead of
guessing which register to read.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c	2026-08-17 14:24:31.197685972 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c	2026-08-17 14:24:31.216609922 +0200
@@ -74,6 +74,11 @@
